Chapter 0 – Before You Begin

This book is a Doctor Who story, and Doctor Who has been running, on and off, since 1963. That is a lot of history to walk in on. You do not need all of it. You need a handful of people, a few places, and one or two very old grudges — and this chapter is here to hand you exactly those, and nothing that would spoil what happens next.

If you already know your Daleks from your Cybermen, feel free to skip ahead. Nobody will mind. Least of all the Doctor, who has never once in his life read the instructions.

The Doctor *

The Doctor is an alien who looks human. He — and sometimes she, but we will get to that — comes from a planet called Gallifrey, and belongs to an ancient, enormously powerful people called the Time Lords.

He has two hearts. He is well over nine hundred years old. He has no name that anyone uses; he simply calls himself "the Doctor", and when people ask "Doctor who?", he smiles and does not answer.

He ran away from home a very long time ago, because the Time Lords believed in watching the universe and never touching it, and he could not bear to only watch. Ever since, he has travelled through time and space helping whoever needs it. He does not carry a gun. He carries a sonic screwdriver — a small device with a glowing tip that can open locks, scan things, and identify almost anything alive — and he relies on being cleverer than whatever is trying to kill him.

He is brilliant, funny, lonely, and much more dangerous than he looks. He is also very bad at goodbyes.

Regeneration *

When a Time Lord is about to die, they can do something extraordinary: their body bursts into golden fire and rebuilds itself from scratch. This is called regeneration.

The Time Lord who walks out of the fire has the same memories, but a brand new face, a new voice, a new body, and quite often a new personality. They can come out older or younger, taller or shorter, a man or a woman. The Doctor has done this many times, and each version is known by number: the Tenth Doctor, the Eleventh Doctor, and so on.

A new face is not designed. It is picked, somehow, from everything the Time Lord has ever seen — and every so often, it turns out to be a face that already belonged to someone. When that happens, the Doctor has learned to take it as a message.

The Doctors in this book *

Three versions of the Doctor matter here, and the book will recognise them by their looks rather than their numbers:

The Tenth Doctor wears a long brown coat and has messy, spiky hair. He is fast-talking, cheerful on the surface, and carries a great deal of guilt underneath.

The Eleventh Doctor wears a bow tie and a tweed jacket. He has "an old young face", and behaves like an excitable child who also happens to be a thousand years old.

The Twelfth Doctor is older, grey-haired, with fierce eyebrows and a sharp temper. He cares enormously and is terrible at showing it.

A Doctor also regenerates at the very start of the book. Who exactly that Doctor is, and why the new face looks the way it does, is part of the story — so we will leave that one to the book.

The TARDIS *

The Doctor's ship is called the TARDIS: Time And Relative Dimension In Space. It can travel anywhere in the universe, at any point in history.

From the outside it looks like a blue British police telephone box from the 1960s. It is supposed to change its disguise to blend in wherever it lands, but that part broke centuries ago, so it has looked like a police box ever since. On the inside it is enormous — the joke everyone makes the first time they step in is "It's bigger on the inside!"

The main room is the console room, where the controls sit around a glowing central column that rises and falls when the ship is flying. When she arrives or leaves, the TARDIS makes a famous wheezing, groaning sound.

The TARDIS is also alive, in her own way. She has moods. She is fond of the Doctor, and he talks to her like an old friend, which is exactly what she is. There is even an old telephone on her console, and a very small number of people have its number.

Companions *

The Doctor almost never travels alone. He takes people with him — usually humans — and they are called his companions. They keep him kind, keep him honest, and occasionally save his life. Two of them matter a great deal in this book.

Rose Tyler was a young shop assistant from a London council estate. She met the Doctor in a basement full of living shop dummies, when he grabbed her hand and said one word: "Run." She travelled with the Ninth and Tenth Doctors, and the two of them loved each other, though he never managed to say it in time.

Their story ended on a beach in Norway. Rose ended up trapped in a parallel universe, sealed off from ours for good, and the Doctor could not follow. She stayed there with a human copy of the Doctor — identical in every way, except that he had only one heart and would grow old like a person. The wall between those two universes is not supposed to be crossable. Ever.

Clara Oswald was a schoolteacher who travelled with the Eleventh and Twelfth Doctors. The Doctor called her "the Impossible Girl", because he kept meeting her in places she could not possibly be — dying, over and over, in different centuries.

The reason was this: to save the Doctor's life, Clara once threw herself into his entire timeline. She was scattered across his whole history in thousands of copies, each one there to protect him at some moment he never noticed. There are echoes of Clara in almost every part of the Doctor's life, including times long before he ever met her.

The Time Lords and Gallifrey *

Gallifrey is the Doctor's home planet: orange skies, silver-leaved trees, and a great domed city called the Citadel. Its people, the Time Lords, see all of time as their responsibility. They are ruled by a High Council and a Lord President, and they are clever, proud, cold, and extremely sure of themselves.

Rassilon was the founder of Time Lord civilisation — the man who first gave his people power over time. He is a legend on Gallifrey, and in the later history of the Time Lords he returned to rule as their President. He wears a metal gauntlet on one hand that can destroy almost anything. He is not a kind man.

Time Lords are sent to the Academy as children, and the Doctor learned many of his skills there. Among other things, Time Lords can touch minds: they can read thoughts by laying their fingers on someone's temples, and with a touch to the forehead they can put a person to sleep.

The Time War *

A long time before the Tenth Doctor's adventures began, the Time Lords fought a war against the Daleks, their greatest enemy. It was fought across all of time and space, and it was so terrible that whole histories were burned away.

For centuries the Doctor believed he ended it by destroying both sides, Gallifrey included — and that he had killed his own people, children and all, to save the rest of the universe. He carried that guilt through several lifetimes.

In fact, he had not. At the last moment, many versions of the Doctor worked together to save Gallifrey instead. They froze the whole planet in a single instant and hid it away, sealed inside a pocket of its own, where nothing could reach it. Every Doctor who took part forgot it afterwards — so for a long time, the Doctor still believed Gallifrey was dead.

A painting records that moment. It hangs in the National Gallery in London, and its title is "Gallifrey Falls No More". It is looked after by an old man known only as the Curator — who has a very familiar face, if you know your Doctors.

The Master *

The Master is a Time Lord too. He and the Doctor were friends as children, at the Academy on Gallifrey. They grew up to be the worst of enemies.

Where the Doctor wants to help, the Master wants to rule. He is brilliant, charming, cruel, and quite possibly mad. Over the centuries he has tried to conquer Earth, destroy the universe, and kill the Doctor more times than either of them can count, and he has worn many faces while doing it. He always comes back.

One meeting between them matters for this book. At the far end of the universe, the Tenth Doctor, his companion Martha Jones, and Captain Jack Harkness — a man from the 51st century who cannot die — met a kind old scientist called Professor Yana, who was trying to save the last humans in existence. Nobody knew that Yana was the Master. He did not know either.

Time Lords have a device called the Chameleon Arch. It rewrites a Time Lord's body into a human one and stores their real self inside an ordinary-looking pocket watch. The Master had used it to hide during the Time War. When he opened the watch, he remembered everything, stole the Doctor's TARDIS, and left him stranded at the end of time. (The Doctor once used the same device himself, to hide as a schoolteacher called John Smith.)

The Daleks and Skaro *

The Daleks are the most feared race in the universe. They are small, hateful, mutated creatures that live inside armoured metal shells, and they believe that anything which is not a Dalek must be destroyed. Their favourite word is "Exterminate". Take the shell away and what is left looks like something soft with tentacles, not unlike a squid or a crab.

They come from a planet called Skaro. Long ago, two peoples there — the Kaleds and the Thals — fought a war that lasted a thousand years, until the planet was a poisoned wasteland. A crippled Kaled scientist named Davros took his own people and turned them into the Daleks.

Davros is one of the Doctor's oldest and most dangerous enemies. When he appears in this book, you should keep that in mind.

Words to keep an eye on *

The Ood are a gentle alien race with a shared, telepathic mind. They can sense things coming across time. They once told the Tenth Doctor that his song was ending soon. Not long after, he was given a stranger warning about the end of his life: "He will knock four times." He had no idea what it meant, and it haunted him.

Fixed points in time are moments in history that must never be changed, no matter what. Most of time can be rewritten; these cannot. The Tenth Doctor once broke one on Mars, out of pride, and deeply regretted it.

Bad Wolf are two words that followed Rose Tyler everywhere she went — scrawled on walls, printed on signs, hidden in names across time and space. In the end it turned out Rose had left them there herself, as a message, after she looked into the heart of the TARDIS and briefly held all of time and space in her head.

Cracks in time and space have shown up before in the Doctor's life, as scars in the wall of the universe that things fall into and are never seen again. Nobody controls them. At least, nobody is supposed to.

Winston Churchill is exactly who you think he is. He has been the Doctor's friend for many years, and he is one of the few people who can telephone the TARDIS.

The Hybrid. The Time Lords have always been terrified of anything that is half one thing and half another. There is an old prophecy on Gallifrey about a hybrid — a creature born of two great races — who will one day stand in the ruins of their world. Nobody quite knows who or what it is.

That is everything you need. The rest, the book will tell you itself.

Allons-y.
